{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nDiscovered from nonblack-brigandish-guillemot: its closing plan review raised this as an advisory finding. ADR 0125 (`docs/adr/0125-opcert-trust-per-environment.md`) replaced repository-pinned op-cert keys with validity-only verification plus an optional per-environment trust list, and the stories under that epic removed the pin file and updated docs and runbooks. User-facing messages in `src` still describe the old model:\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/env_identity.py:176`, the re-identify warning (`reidentification_warning`), says `verify.require_environment` \"trusts only the own or pinned keys of the environment it names\".\n- `src/rebar/_store/env_identity.py:64` (the `.opcert-key` entry of `CARRY_OVER`, rendered into that same warning) says old-key attestations \"fail as `mismatch` unless that key is pinned\".\n\nNo story's scope or grep check covered `src` prose, so these survived. A sweep of `src` for the same stale model (`git grep -n -i -E 'pinned|out-of-band' -- src`, read hit by hit) found more op-cert sites, all developer-facing:\n\n- `src/rebar/attest/opcert.py:6`, the module docstring, says anyone verifies an op-cert \"against that environment's out-of-band-pinned public key\" (lines 12-15 of the same docstring already note ADR 0125).\n- `src/rebar/attest/opcert.py:348`, the `opcert_from_record` docstring, says the envelope \"is fed to verify_opcert with a pinned keyring\"; the production consumer `verify_opcert_record` (`src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py:170`) verifies it on the key embedded in its signature.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_serving.py:203`, a comment, says it exposes \"pinned-key match status\" through `/health`; the status comes from `opcert_signing_status` (`src/rebar/_mcp_opcert_health.py:21`), which compares the bound signer against `verify.trusted_opcert_keys`.\n- `src/rebar/mcp_server.py:532`, the `compose_startup_opcert_binding` docstring, says \"the pinned environment's startup signer\".\n- `src/rebar/_opcert_binding.py:112`, a bug note, says the op-cert \"claimed the pinned production environment\".\n\nOne unrelated docstring also matches the ticket's own grep check: `src/rebar/_mcp_derive.py:49` (\"A pinned keyword argument\"), so it is reworded too (\"A fixed keyword argument\"). The remaining `pinned` hits in `src` are about other things and stay: the pinned SSHSIG namespace and scheme (`attest/opcert.py:160`, `attest/registry.py`), pinned snapshots and refs, pinned policy tables, pinned test wording, and authorship keys.\n\n## Approach\n\nRewrite the 4 sites to state the ADR 0125 model, matching the verifier's actual behaviour in `src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py` (`verify_opcert_record`, lines 170-310): a certificate verifies on the public key embedded in its signature (trust basis `envelope_key`); when the verifying environment sets `verify.trusted_opcert_keys`, the (environment, key) pair must be listed or the verdict is `untrusted`; `verify.require_environment` turns a certificate naming another environment `foreign_key` and requires a trust list.\n\n- Warning (line ~176): old attestations still certify on the embedded key, because verification checks validity only by default; an environment that sets `verify.trusted_opcert_keys` trusts them only while its list names the old environment-and-key pair (otherwise they read `untrusted`); `verify.require_environment` turns a certificate naming another environment `foreign_key`.\n- `.opcert-key` carry-over (line ~64): old-key attestations still verify on the key embedded in each signature, but an environment whose `verify.trusted_opcert_keys` list omits the old environment-and-key pair reads them as `untrusted`.\n- `attest/opcert.py:6`: \"anyone verifies it against the public key embedded in its signature, checking only validity unless the verifier keeps its own trust list (ADR 0125)\".\n- `attest/opcert.py:348`: \"Callers verify the returned envelope on the key embedded in its signature (verify_opcert_record, ADR 0125)\".\n- `_mcp_serving.py:203`: \"Expose the bound signer's trust-list match status through /health; warn, never abort.\"\n- `mcp_server.py:532`: \"the claimed environment's startup signer\"; `_opcert_binding.py:112`: \"claimed the production environment\".\n- `_mcp_derive.py:49`: \"A fixed keyword argument\".\n\nProse-only change: no behaviour, config key, env var or flag changes.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/env_identity.py` — the 2 user-facing messages.\n- `src/rebar/attest/opcert.py` — module docstring opening sentence and the `opcert_from_record` docstring.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_serving.py` — the `/health` status comment.\n- `src/rebar/mcp_server.py` — the `compose_startup_opcert_binding` docstring.\n- `src/rebar/_opcert_binding.py` — the bug note in `spawn_context_daemon`.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_derive.py` — the `PerCall` docstring, so the grep check is exact.\n- `tests/interfaces/store/test_env_identity_guard.py` — extend `_assert_states_the_current_attestation_policy` to require the ADR 0125 terms and reject the pinned-key wording.\n\n## Testing\n\n`_assert_states_the_current_attestation_policy` (used by `test_reidentification_warning_says_old_attestations_still_certify`) currently checks only `verify.require_environment` and `foreign_key`. Extend it to assert `verify.trusted_opcert_keys` and `untrusted` appear and that `pinned` does not; this fails on the current wording and passes after the fix. Run `pytest tests/interfaces/store/test_env_identity_guard.py`, then the full `make verify` before push.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `reidentification_warning({\"x\"}, acknowledged=False)` contains `verify.trusted_opcert_keys`, `untrusted`, `verify.require_environment` and `foreign_key`, and does not contain `pinned`; checked by `_assert_states_the_current_attestation_policy` in `tests/interfaces/store/test_env_identity_guard.py`.\n- [ ] `git grep -n -i 'pinned key\\|own or pinned\\|unless that key is pinned' -- src` prints nothing.\n- [ ] `git grep -n -i -e 'out-of-band-pinned\\*\\* public' -e 'pinned-key match' -e 'pinned keyring' -e \"pinned environment's\" -e 'pinned production environment' -- src` prints nothing.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/interfaces/store/test_env_identity_guard.py` passes; the extended assertion fails against the pre-fix wording."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795229322438965001,"uuid":"0cb1992b-3e70-4c58-bb62-4ed6add00a2e"}